2.0	Escherichia coli Antimicrobial Resistance in Nigeria by Setting
E. coli is a commonly used indicator species for monitoring antimicrobial resistance (AMR) in all sectors of activity including humans, animals and the environment. All three sectors are reported to have high levels of multidrug resistance (MDR), increasingly plasmid-mediated colistin resistance (PMCR), and emerging extended-spectrum β-lactamase (ESBL) producers in Nigeria. Most of the evidence is found in cross-sectional, single-site surveys, mostly phenotypic, and a smaller but increasing number of sequencing studies. This section outlines evidence for the human, clinical and community setting, for animals and the food chain, and for the environment, and then collates patterns that appear in more than one of these settings.
2.1 Human Clinical and Community Settings

In Nigerian hospitals and communities alike, E. coli is consistently resistant to the most commonly prescribed oral agents; ampicillin, amoxicillin–clavulanate, tetracycline, sulfonamides, trimethoprim and fluoroquinolones; while resistance to last-line agents remains less prevalent, though it does occur (Hertz et al., 2019; Perewari et al.,2022 ; Mohammed & Kurawa, 2025). This burden is shared by both diarrhoeagenic and extraintestinal lineages, and blaCTX-M-type ESBL genes have been detected in humans, animals and the environment (Anueyiagu et al., 2024).
Reported ESBL prevalence among clinical E. coli isolates differs substantially across centers. Between August and December 2024 at Aminu Kano Teaching Hospital, 71 E. coli isolates were recovered from 500 suspected urinary tract infection (UTI) samples. Although 67.6% tested positive on initial ESBL screening, double-disc synergy testing confirmed production in only 15.5% (11/71). All 11 confirmed isolates remained susceptible to imipenem. Resistance to nitrofurantoin reached 54.5%, and one isolate grew on colistin-supplemented agar (Mohammed & Kurawa, 2025). A much lower rate was observed in Aba, Abia State, where 350 clinical and nonclinical samples yielded 83 confirmed E. coli isolates. Multidrug resistance was frequent in both clinical (58.1%) and nonclinical (53.9%) isolates, and five-class resistance occurred more often in the clinical group (19.4% vs. 5.8%). Even so, phenotypic ESBL production was only 1.2%, and none of the isolates harbored blaTEM, blaSHV, or blaCTX-M (Ajuga et al., 2021). By contrast, smaller series have recorded higher proportions. In Bauchi, 8 of 15 E. coli isolates (53%) collected in 2015 were ESBL-positive, alongside widespread resistance to trimethoprim (13/15), sulfonamides (13/15), and ciprofloxacin (10/15) (Hertz et al., 2019). High ESBL prevalence was likewise reported among inpatients with UTI in Ogbomoso (Yakub et al., 2025).
Much of this variation across Nigerian studies comes down to laboratory methods. Presumptive screening routinely produces higher estimates than confirmatory testing, as in Kano (67.6% vs. 15.5%) and Abuja, where 47 of 54 phenotypic ESBL producers were confirmed (Medugu et al., 2022). Mohammed & Kurawa (2025) attributed the Kano discrepancy in part to co-production of other β-lactamases that interfere with confirmatory assays. Molecular testing also tends to give lower rates than phenotypic methods, and gene panels restricted to blaTEM, blaSHV, and blaCTX-M can miss other ESBL genes; in Aba, the low ESBL rate coincided with the absence of all three targets (Ajuga et al., 2021). Patient populations and prior antibiotic exposure also affect reported rates. Resistance is generally higher in hospital than in community settings, especially in complicated UTIs or recurrent infections treated with multiple antibiotic courses (Anueyiagu et al., 2024). That contrast appears between Abuja, where 50% of 107 tertiary-hospital isolates showed an ESBL phenotype (Medugu et al., 2022), and Lagos, where cephalosporin resistance ranged from 1.6% to 15% among 247 community isolates (Adenipekun et al., 2016), though the two studies used different endpoints and cannot be compared directly. Study design and sample size limit comparisons as well. Small or preliminary collections like the 15-isolate Bauchi series cannot support prevalence estimates (Hertz et al., 2019), and Mohammed & Kurawa (2025) pointed to sample size, population characteristics, and testing protocols as key sources of disagreement between reports. Rising global ESBL figures have been linked to wider awareness and improved detection as well as a real increase in ESBL-producing strains (Ajuga et al., 2021), which may also explain more recent Nigerian data. Since few Nigerian studies have evaluated these factors side by side, they are best read as likely contributors rather than quantified determinants.

Comparisons between clinical and nonclinical isolates from the same region show that resistance gradients vary by drug class, while genomic data point to overlap between community and hospital strains. In Port Harcourt, Rivers State, Perewari et al. (2022) recovered presumptive E. coli from 78% of stool, 68% of urine, 44% of poultry, and 26% of soil samples. Out of 108 presumptive isolates, 80.7% were resistant to tetracycline, and tetA was the most common determinant (89.9%) in the 89 isolates confirmed as E. coli by 16S rRNA PCR. Ceftazidime resistance was higher in clinical stool (41.0%) and urine (35.3%) than in poultry (13.6%) or soil (0%). Cefuroxime resistance followed a different pattern; it was high in poultry (95.5%) and urine (75.5%), but depicted lower rates in soil (46.2%) and stool (18.7%) (Perewari et al., 2022). In Edo State, Jesumirhewe et al. (2023) recovered 46 E. coli isolates from healthy students, hospital wounds, and poultry, including four ESBL-producing human isolates (three wound and one nasal). One student nasal isolate and two teaching-hospital wound isolates belonged to ST1722 and differed by a single core-genome allele, supporting a possible clonal spread within the human population (Jesumirhewe et al., 2023).

On the other hand, hospital-based studies show a heavier resistance burden, though reserve agents still retain activity in several settings. Between March 2019 and September 2020 at a tertiary hospital in Abuja, 95.3% of 107 clinical E. coli isolates were MDR and 80.4% were resistant to ceftriaxone; blaCTX-M-15 was present in 70.1%, and ST131 was the dominant lineage (21.5%) (Medugu et al., 2022). Carbapenem resistance was equally observed in these , with 7.5% resistant to meropenem and 5.6% carrying blaNDM-1 or blaNDM-5 genes (Medugu et al., 2022). Smaller clinical collections have reported better preservation of reserve drugs. No carbapenem resistance was detected in Bauchi, where isolates were noted to be  susceptible to nitrofurantoin (Hertz et al., 2019). In Kano, all confirmed ESBL producers remained susceptible to imipenem, although more than half were resistant to nitrofurantoin (Mohammed & Kurawa, 2025). Since,  the single case of colistin-agar growth in Kano was not followed by broth microdilution or mcr genotyping, confirmatory phenotypic and molecular testing is still needed to establish polymyxin resistance in clinical E. coli.
2.2 Animal, Abattoir, and Food-Chain Settings
The resistance profiles of E.coli across studies reflects of the heavy antimicrobial use in livestock production in Nigeria. In a systematic review of cattle and poultry production across sub-Saharan Africa, Nigeria accounted for 9 of 24 eligible studies, with farm-level usage ranging from 67% to 100% and dominated by tetracyclines, β-lactams/aminoglycosides, and fluoroquinolones (Azabo et al., 2022).
Early baseline data from south-western Nigeria reflect that selection pressure. Across eight poultry farms in Oyo and Osun States sampled in 2008, Adelowo et al. (2014) recovered 36 E. coli isolates from waste, litter, soil, and water, with frequent resistance to tetracycline (81%), sulfamethoxazole (67%), streptomycin (56%), trimethoprim (47%), ciprofloxacin (42%), and ampicillin (36%), alongside blaTEM in 11 of 13 ampicillin-resistant isolates (85%) and class 1 and 2 integrons in 5 and 6 isolates, respectively. Notably, those 2008 isolates remained susceptible to amoxicillin–clavulanate, cefotaxime, ceftiofur, and colistin. Even in that earlier survey, trimethoprim and ciprofloxacin resistance was observed  on farms reporting no use of either drug, pointing to unrecorded feed additives or co-selection as possible  additional drivers (Adelowo et al., 2014).
Surveys of healthy food animals show a similar pattern, with clear shifts between earlier and more recent sampling. Between December 2012 and June 2013, Adenipekun et al. (2015) recovered E. coli from 81–90% of 600 faecal samples from cattle, chickens, and swine in Lagos. Among 211 β-lactamase-positive isolates, resistance was highest to tetracycline (58.8%), trimethoprim–sulfamethoxazole (39.8%), and ampicillin (34.1%). About 40% were pan-susceptible, and none were resistant to amikacin, cefepime, ceftazidime, or carbapenems. Even in that earlier survey, MDR was more frequent in chickens (61.3%) than in cattle (40.8%), and identical pulsed-field gel electrophoresis patterns across species showed that some lineages already circulated between hosts (Adenipekun et al., 2015). Later poultry sampling in Ilorin in 2019 captured ESBL-producing E. coli in 10.5% of 354 caecal samples (Al-Mustapha et al., 2023). All ESBL producers were MDR and 5.4% were resistant to colistin, though none were resistant to meropenem. Sequencing of 11 isolates revealed diverse sequence types, predominantly IncF plasmids, and blaCTX-M-15 as the primary ESBL gene. More broadly, poultry tend to carry higher ESBL-E. coli loads than cattle or swine, driven by β-lactam use, contaminated housing environments, and co-selection from feed additives such as copper and zinc (Widodo et al., 2024). In Edo State, healthy poultry and people from the same locality shared an ST155 lineage that differed by three alleles or fewer (Jesumirhewe et al., 2023). The aforementioned close match provides direct genetic evidence connecting livestock and human reservoirs.
Abattoirs bring animals, workers, and meat into close contact. Between January and April 2016 at an abattoir in Awka, Ugwu et al. (2018) recovered 60 E. coli isolates from 100 meat and vendor-table swabs; most were resistant to cefuroxime (91%) and amoxicillin–clavulanate (88%), while 12% produced ESBLs and 10% produced metallo-β-lactamases. Across two abattoirs in Abuja and Lagos in 2020, Aworh et al. (2022) recovered ESBL-producing E. coli from 97 of 448 samples (21.7%), made up of 33.9% of workers (40/118), 22.4% of environmental specimens (13/58), and 16.2% of slaughtered cattle (44/272). Nearly all isolates (99%) exhibited MDR, with widespread resistance to ciprofloxacin (95.9%), ceftazidime (89.7%), and tetracycline (82.5%), yet every isolate remained susceptible to carbapenems, cefoxitin, and nitrofurantoin. Whole-genome sequencing detected blaCTX-M-15 in 90.7% of isolates and blaCTX-M-14 in 5.2%, along with the first reports of blaCTX-M-55 in humans and cattle in Nigeria. The sequenced collection also included clonally related ST46 isolates from humans and cattle, plus an ST205 isolate from a Lagos worker carrying both mcr-1.1 and blaCTX-M-15. Aworh et al. (2022) linked this high carriage to over-the-counter availability of human and veterinary antimicrobials in Nigeria, in contrast to prescription-only supply in Europe, where lower ESBL-E. coli prevalence has been reported in cattle and farm workers.

2.3 Environmental Settings
Nigerian soils and surface waters receive untreated human and animal waste and serve as both reservoirs and exposure routes for resistant E. coli. Resistance to older β-lactams, tetracyclines, and sulfonamides was documented early on in poultry-farm soils and litter (Adelowo et al., 2014). In a later survey in Rivers State, soil isolates showed 100% resistance to tetracycline and amoxicillin–clavulanate (Perewari et al., 2022). Surface waters carry a heavy ESBL burden as well. From the Asa River in Ilorin, 58 of 84 E. coli isolates (69.0%) were ESBL producers; 11 of 16 PCR-tested isolates carried blaTEM, blaCTX-M, or both, and curing assays pointed to plasmid-mediated resistance (Olatunji et al., 2016). Over a year-long survey of Ojerame Dam and Ovokoto Spring in Edo State, Beshiru et al. (2024) recovered 114 E. coli isolates from 84 water samples. Forty-nine were ESBL producers resistant to ampicillin, cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, and ceftazidime, while 65 were non-ESBL producers, yet MDR across three to eight antimicrobial classes occurred in both groups. All isolates remained susceptible to carbapenems and nitrofurantoin, and the detected resistance genes included blaTEM, blaCTX-M-1, blaCTX-M-15, tetA, tetM, sul1–3, qnrA, qnrB, and qnrS (Beshiru et al., 2024).
Wastewater from institutions and abattoirs carries heavy loads of resistant E. coli. Between July 2017 and March 2018, Emurotu et al. (2024) recovered 906 presumptive uropathogenic E. coli isolates from hostel drains at the University of Nigeria, Nsukka (UNN, Enugu State) and Kogi State University, Anyigba (Kogi State), as well as from the UNN wastewater treatment plant. Among 63 isolates tested for susceptibility, all were resistant to amoxicillin–clavulanate, with frequent resistance to streptomycin (88.9%), ampicillin (69.8%), tetracycline (66.7%), and gentamicin (65.1%), though all remained susceptible to meropenem. Of 21 isolates screened by PCR, 8 (38.1%) carried blaCTX-M and 8 (38.1%) carried blaOXA-1 (Emurotu et al., 2024). In Calabar South, Agashi et al. (2025) obtained 437 E. coli isolates from 700 abattoir wastewater samples (62.4%). Resistance approached 100% for ampicillin (96–100%) and amoxicillin–clavulanate (95–100%) and stayed high for trimethoprim–sulfamethoxazole (80–94%), ceftazidime (79–88%), and cefuroxime (77–98%), whereas ofloxacin was the most active agent (87–96% susceptibility). The 69–85% imipenem resistance recorded on disc diffusion in that study still requires confirmation by MIC testing or carbapenemase detection, because other Nigerian abattoir and surface-water surveys have reported carbapenem susceptibility (Aworh et al., 2022; Beshiru et al., 2024).
Household water offers a direct route of community exposure. In peri-urban Ibadan, Rabiu et al. (2025) sampled 66 wells and 30 boreholes in 2019–2020 and characterised 25 E. coli isolates recovered from 13 wells. Fifteen (60%) were MDR, and overall resistance was highest to ampicillin (76%) and sulfonamides (48%). Whole-genome sequencing identified 15 sequence types, blaTEM-1B, blaCTX-M variants, plasmid-mediated quinolone resistance genes, and one mcr-1-carrying isolate. Isolates of the same sequence type from neighbouring homes roughly 500 m apart differed by few or no single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs), and ampicillin resistance transferred by conjugation from two of six MDR isolates to a recipient E. coli strain (Rabiu et al., 2025).
2.4 Patterns Across Settings
Across human, animal, and environmental settings in Nigeria, E. coli displays a broadly consistent resistance profile: high resistance to ampicillin, amoxicillin–clavulanate, tetracycline, sulfonamides, and trimethoprim, alongside growing resistance to fluoroquinolones and early-generation cephalosporins. Carbapenems remain largely active, although meropenem resistance and blaNDM genes have been reported in clinical isolates from Abuja (Medugu et al., 2022). ESBL production is widespread but variable, ranging from about 3% to 44% in confirmed clinical series and from 10% to 69% in animal and environmental collections. Part of that range is methodological rather than biological. Studies that plated samples onto cefotaxime- or ESBL-selective media report prevalence per sample (Aworh et al., 2022; Al-Mustapha et al., 2023; Beshiru et al., 2024), whereas others report the proportion of recovered E. coli isolates that produce ESBLs (Olatunji et al., 2016; Ugwu et al., 2018), so the two sets of figures are not directly interchangeable.
Where molecular data are available, blaCTX-M-15 is the dominant ESBL gene across sequencing studies (Aworh et al., 2022; Al-Mustapha et al., 2023; Jesumirhewe et al., 2023; Medugu et al., 2022; Rabiu et al., 2025), whereas PCR screening of Edo State water isolates detected blaCTX-M-1 (89.8%) ahead of blaCTX-M-15 (77.6%) (Beshiru et al., 2024). Colistin resistance is also beginning to appear across reservoirs, with mcr-1 detected in abattoir and household-water isolates and phenotypic resistance reported in poultry, alongside an unconfirmed colistin-agar report in clinical isolates (Aworh et al., 2022; Al-Mustapha et al., 2023; Rabiu et al., 2025; Mohammed & Kurawa, 2025). Sequencing studies also reveal overlapping lineages and mobile elements across compartments. In Edo State, Jesumirhewe et al. (2023) identified closely related ST155 isolates in healthy people and poultry, as well as ST1722 in a healthy individual's nasal sample and two clinical wounds, pointing to shared occurrence across community and clinical groups rather than proven transmission. Elsewhere, Aworh et al. (2022) recovered clonally related ST46 isolates from abattoir workers and cattle, and Rabiu et al. (2025) demonstrated transferable resistance plasmids in household water in Ibadan.
Even so, most of this evidence comes from single-site phenotypic surveys that used varied antibiotic panels, breakpoints, and sampling periods spanning 2008 to 2024, and only five applied whole-genome sequencing. Geographic coverage is uneven as well, with the north-east and north-west each represented by a single clinical study. The resistance genes, plasmids, and lineages underlying these phenotypes across Nigerian settings therefore remain only partly characterised. This gap motivates a closer look at resistance genes in Section 3 and an analysis of publicly available Nigerian E. coli genomes in Sections 4 and 5.

